	In problems 4#, do the following:

	> a) Find the matrix for the given transformation $T$.

	> b) Determine if $T$ is one-to-one. If it is not, find a basis for $\ker T$.

	> c) Determine if $T$ is onto and find a basis for $\image T$.

	> d) If $T$ is invertible, find a formula for $T^{-1}$.

	#.

	$$
		T\left( [[ x ; y ]] \right) = [[ -2x + y ; -5x + 3y ]].
	$$

	#.

	$$
		T\left( [[ a ; b ; c ; d ]] \right) = [[ a + 2b - c ; 2b + c - d ]].
	$$

	#.

	$$
		T\left( [[ x ; y ; z ]] \right) = [[ 2x + y - z ; -3x - y ; y - 3z ]].
	$$

	#.

	$$
		T\left( x \right) = [[ x ; 2x ; 3x ; 0 ]].
	$$

	- - -

	Problems 4# concern the following situation. Suppose when pizzas sell for $p$ dollars each, New Haven residents want to buy
	$$
		q = 10 - p + d
	$$
	
	thousand pizzas per week, and pizza restaurants want to sell
	
	$$
		q = p - 2 + s
	$$

	thousand pizzas per week. The market settles at the equilibrium price, i.e. the one where these two expressions for $q$ are equal. The variables $d$ and $s$ are called **shocks**. If $d > 0$, buyers demand $d$ thousand more pizzas than usual, regardless of price (for example, because a new restaurant opened). If $s > 0$, restaurants are willing to sell $s$ thousand more pizzas, regardless of price. Both $d$ and $s$ can be negative.

	#. Find the equilibrium price $p(d, s)$ and quantity $q(d, s)$ of pizzas sold (in thousands), both in terms of $d$ and $s$.
	
	#. Let $T : #R#^2 \to #R#^2$ be given by
	
	$$
		T\left( [[ d ; s ]] \right) = \left[ \begin{array}{c} p(d, s) - p(0, 0) \\ q(d, s) - q(0, 0) \end{array} \right],
	$$
	
	i.e. the change in the equilibrium price and quantity when there are shocks $d$ and $s$. Find the matrix $A$ for $T$, and then find $A^{-1}$.
	
	#. Economists typically can't observe shocks directly, only prices and quantities. If the price of pizza in New Haven rose by $\$1$ last week and the number of pizzas sold fell by 3 thousand, what shocks occurred?
	
	#. Suppose you only have data on quantities, not prices. Let $Q : #R#^2 \to #R#$ be defined by
	
	$$
		Q\left( [[ d ; s ]] \right) = q(d, s) - q(0, 0),
	$$
	
	so its matrix is just the second row of the matrix for $T$. Find a basis for $\ker Q$. Explain in context what the kernel means, and specifically why it is a problem for determining shocks.

